# 12. Calico Routing Investigation

On the master:

```bash
ip route | grep 192.168
```

We found:

```text
192.168.125.64/26 via 10.0.0.5 dev tunl0 proto bird onlink
```

This is important.

It means the master was routing traffic for the worker's pod CIDR through:

```text
tunl0
   |
   v
10.0.0.5
```

Calico was therefore creating the expected IPIP route.

We also saw:

```text
blackhole 192.168.167.192/26 proto bird
```

and individual Calico interfaces:

```text
192.168.167.193 dev cali...
192.168.167.194 dev cali...
192.168.167.195 dev cali...
```

These were Calico workload interfaces.

---

# 13. Reverse Path Filtering Investigation

We checked:

```bash
sysctl net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter
sysctl net.ipv4.conf.tunl0.rp_filter
sysctl net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter
```

Initially:

```text
all.rp_filter = 0
tunl0.rp_filter = 1
default.rp_filter = 1
```

Because Calico IPIP can be affected by strict reverse-path filtering, we temporarily changed:

```bash
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.tunl0.rp_filter=0
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter=0
```

We retested:

```bash
curl -vk --connect-timeout 5 \
https://192.168.125.76:8443
```

It still timed out from the master.

Therefore:

```text
rp_filter
```

was not the final cause of the browser 404.

This was an important troubleshooting lesson:

> Do not keep changing networking parameters after the evidence shows that the actual application problem is elsewhere.

