# 17. Effective NSG Investigation

The correct command in this Azure CLI version was:

```bash
az network nic list-effective-nsg \
-g dev \
-n k8-m1515_z1 \
-o json
```

and:

```bash
az network nic list-effective-nsg \
-g dev \
-n k8-worker341_z1 \
-o json
```

Important worker NSG rules included:

```text
Allow-K8s-Ingress-31347
priority 310
TCP
Inbound
Internet -> port 31347
```

and:

```text
Allow-K8s-Node-Internal
priority 400
All
Inbound
VirtualNetwork -> VirtualNetwork
0-65535
```

Azure's default rules also included:

```text
AllowVnetInBound
AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound
DenyAllInBound
```

The important internal rule was:

```text
Allow-K8s-Node-Internal
```

which allowed VirtualNetwork traffic between the nodes.

So Azure NSG was not the explanation for the final HTTP 404.

