# 22. Final Root Cause

There were several networking issues investigated during troubleshooting, but they were **not the final reason Chrome displayed 404**.

The final HTTP 404 was caused by the Ingress routing configuration/state.

### Original configuration

```text
Host = html.local
```

But Chrome accessed:

```text
http://20.15.171.215
```

Therefore:

```text
Host: 20.15.171.215
```

did not match:

```text
Host: html.local
```

NGINX returned:

```text
404 Not Found
```

At another point, the Ingress resource itself was absent:

```text
kubectl get ingress -A
No resources found
```

which also caused NGINX to have no application routing rule.

After recreating the Ingress with:

```text
HOSTS *
```

the request matched and reached the service.

---

# 23. What Was NOT the Final Problem

We investigated these areas:

### Calico

Calico was running:

```text
calico-node master  = Running
calico-node worker  = Running
```

Calico IPIP routes were present.

### Pod networking

Worker could directly reach:

```text
192.168.125.76:8443
```

and complete TLS.

### NGINX controller

Controller was:

```text
1/1 Running
```

and successfully routed requests to:

```text
html-service
```

### Azure NSG

The effective NSG contained:

```text
Allow-K8s-Node-Internal
VirtualNetwork -> VirtualNetwork
All ports
```

So normal internal node communication was allowed.

### rp_filter

We tested disabling strict reverse-path filtering, but the master-to-controller timeout remained.

Therefore it was not necessary for solving the final HTTP 404.

### Chrome

Chrome itself was not the problem.

Chrome was simply sending:

```text
Host: 20.15.171.215
```

which did not match the original:

```text
html.local
```

host rule.

---

# 24. Admission Webhook Problem

There was also a genuine separate problem:

```text
ingress-nginx-admission
```

The validating webhook was timing out.

Error:

```text
failed calling webhook
validate.nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io
context deadline exceeded
```

The webhook service was:

```text
ingress-nginx-controller-admission
```

with:

```text
ClusterIP: 10.107.49.47
Port: 443
```

and endpoint:

```text
192.168.125.76:8443
```

The master could not connect to that endpoint.

This prevented commands such as:

```bash
kubectl apply -f html-ingress.yaml
```

and:

```bash
kubectl patch ingress ...
```

from completing.

So there were actually **two different issues**:

```text
Issue 1:
Ingress host/routing configuration
        |
        v
HTTP 404

Issue 2:
NGINX admission webhook connectivity
        |
        v
kubectl apply/patch fails
```

These should not be confused with each other.

